$DIR/main.p:40:10: cannot pass h.String to callf, which keeps it after returning
    h.String is a function literal that uses x. A function literal that uses
    variables of the function around it refers to those variables where they
    live, in f's frame (x is declared at main.p:38). callf passes a pointer
    loaded from *g to g (which may call (HF).String) (main.p:34), which stores h
    in package variable keepF (main.p:27), so h.String would outlive x.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Change callf to take a value or an owner, which it can keep: an owned[T]
    from new, a shared[T], or a library owner such as a vec.Vec.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3003" for a longer explanation)
